# DayReel Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values
# NEVER commit .env to git

# AWS Configuration
# REQUIRED — these are no longer optional. There is no local AWS emulator any
# more; S3 and DynamoDB calls go to a real AWS account. Use an IAM user scoped
# to the three buckets and the one table below.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# S3 Buckets
S3_RAW_BUCKET=dayreel-raw-videos
S3_PROCESSED_BUCKET=dayreel-processed
S3_HLS_BUCKET=dayreel-hls-output

# DynamoDB
DYNAMODB_TABLE=dayreel-jobs

# Queue Names
# The pipeline routes on these names. On the SQLite driver they are values of
# the `queue` column; on the SQS driver they are the SQS queue names that
# scripts/aws-sqs-setup.sh creates. Either way they mirror the constants in
# backend/internal/events/messages.go.
QUEUE_VALIDATE=dayreel-validate
QUEUE_EXTRACT=dayreel-extract
QUEUE_TRANSCRIBE=dayreel-transcribe
QUEUE_PACKAGE=dayreel-package
QUEUE_DLQ=dayreel-dlq

# Queue Driver
# Which broker the API and workers use: `sqlite` or `sqs`. Anything else is
# refused at startup rather than falling back, because a typo that silently
# selected the default would give you a deployment convinced it is on SQS while
# writing messages into a local file no other host can see.
#
# sqlite (default) — one file, no account, no per-request cost. The workers must
#   share a filesystem with the API, which is the whole constraint.
# sqs — pick this when workers run on more than one machine, when the queue must
#   survive the box, or when AWS should hold messages while every worker is down
#   for a deploy. It costs money per request: an idle worker long-polls once
#   every 20s per stage, and each of those is a billed call. Run
#   `make sqs-setup` first — unlike SQLite, the queues do not create themselves.
QUEUE_DRIVER=sqlite

# Queue Engine — SQLite driver only
# Ignored when QUEUE_DRIVER=sqs: there is no file.
#
# Path to the queue database. Created on first start; delete it with
# `make queue-reset`. Keep it out of git — .gitignore covers data/.
QUEUE_DB_PATH=./data/queue.db

# Queue Engine — how long a receive waits for work
# Leave this UNSET unless you have a reason: the default follows the driver,
# because the same number means two different things. On sqlite it is a local
# ticker's cadence and 250ms costs four queries a second against a file. On sqs
# it is WaitTimeSeconds, whose granularity is one second — 250ms there would be
# a short poll that returns instantly and is billed per call, so the sqs default
# is 20s (SQS's maximum long poll, and the only value that keeps four idle
# workers inside the free tier).
#
#   sqlite default: 250ms
#   sqs default:    20s
#
# Left commented out on purpose. Setting it here would pin 250ms across a switch
# to QUEUE_DRIVER=sqs, which is exactly the case the driver-shaped default
# exists to avoid.
#QUEUE_POLL_INTERVAL=250ms

# Queue Engine — both drivers
# On SQS these two must match what scripts/aws-sqs-setup.sh wrote onto the
# queues. AWS enforces the queue's own attributes; these are only what the
# workers believe, and a queue enforcing a 5m lease against workers that think
# they have 10 fails in the least obvious way possible.
#
# How long a claimed message stays invisible to other workers. Must outlive the
# slowest stage: transcribe is the risk, and it heartbeats rather than relying
# on a longer timeout.
QUEUE_VISIBILITY_TIMEOUT=5m
# Delivery attempts before a worker dead-letters the message to QUEUE_DLQ. On
# SQS this is also written into each queue's redrive policy as maxReceiveCount —
# but that policy is a safety net, not the mechanism: the worker dead-letters
# explicitly, and does so immediately for failures that can never succeed.
QUEUE_MAX_DELIVERIES=3

# API Server
API_PORT=8080

# Uploads
# Multipart part size in bytes. S3's minimum is 5 MiB (5242880) and anything
# smaller is rejected — not at upload time, but at CompleteMultipartUpload with
# EntityTooSmall, which fails the whole job after every part has "succeeded".
# Leave at the floor unless you have a reason. To exercise the multipart path
# at all, use a test clip larger than 5 MiB.
UPLOAD_PART_SIZE=5242880

# Worker Configuration
# One binary serves every stage; WORKER_STAGE selects which, and is set per
# invocation by the Makefile (`make worker-extract`), not usually here.
WORKER_STAGE=
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2
# Mock is the default. The budget rules in config/free-tier.md allow only a
# handful of real runs, so every stage downstream of transcribe is developed
# against synthetic cues. Set to false only for a deliberate run.
MOCK_TRANSCRIBE=true
# Where the ggml model file lives when MOCK_TRANSCRIBE=false. It is downloaded
# rather than committed (a few hundred MB); .gitignore covers models/. This was
# a container volume mount when the workers ran in Docker — now it is just a
# path on the machine running the worker.
WHISPER_MODEL_PATH=./models/ggml-base.bin

# HLS Delivery
# Reels are served directly from the HLS bucket — no CDN. The bucket must be
# readable by the player, and CORS-configured for range requests.
HLS_BASE_URL=https://dayreel-hls-output.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com

# The base URL a player or mobile client uses to reach S3 objects, when it
# differs from the endpoint the backend signs against. It existed because the
# emulator's in-cluster hostname did not resolve outside the compose network.
# Against real AWS the two are the same, so leave it empty; set it only if you
# put a proxy in front of S3.
S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=
